      "Language": "rus",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To assess short- and long-term effects of amiodaron on atrioventricular conduction (AVC) of the heart."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIAL AND METHODS",
          "content": "Amiodaron was given to 477 patients (mean age 48.7 +/- 0.7 years) with various arrhythmias caused, as a rule by coronary heart disease. A mean saturation dose was 809.4 +/- 13.4 mg/day, a mean maintanence dose--263.5 +/- 5.4 mg/day. Mean follow-up 20.85 +/- 1.2 months. The frequency of AVC disorders was estimated at regular Holter monitoring."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In saturation, atrioventricular block of the first degree arose in 17.2% patients, of the second and third degree--only in one patient. AVC disorders disappeared at reduction of amiodaron dose. Only in one patient the drug was discontinued because of recurrent block of the third degree and bradycardia."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In saturation, ECG records lengthening of P-R intervals by 18.9%, on the average. Maintenance therapy increases the length of P-R interval by 8.1%, on the average."
        }
      ]},
